{"type": "message", "role": "assistant", "content": "<think>\nExcellent! The diagram image (x6) contains \"T\u00eate de pompe Volume nominal : 10 ml\"! So the nominal volume is in the diagram image. And x10 is the approval stamp with date 22/01/2026 \u2192 date_validation = 2026-01-22! That matches doc-002's answer (2026-01-22).\n\nSo images matter a lot:\n- Diagram image contains nominal volume (and maybe other values).\n- Approval stamp image contains validation date.\n- Motor rating plate contains motor info (not needed).\n\nSo the workflow must OCR images. The model has vision. Great.\n\nBut careful: the hidden test may have different layouts. Using the model's vision on all images is a good approach.\n\nNow, this is a substantial task. Let me design:\n\nApproach: For each PDF:\n1. Extract text per page (pymupdf).\n2. Extract images (xrefs) per page, render to PNG, and OCR them with the vision model (or include them in the main extraction call).\n3. Build a combined \"document representation\": text + image OCR results, with page markers.\n4. Send to the LLM with a detailed extraction prompt, asking for the 15 fields with reasoning.\n5. Post-process and validate.\n\nGiven 35 docs and 15 test docs, and 2h budget, LLM calls are feasible. Each doc has ~3-5 images. That's ~150 image OCR calls for training. Could be slow. Better: include images directly in the main extraction call (multimodal), so one call per doc. But images can be large; 8GB GPU, model context. Let's try one call per doc with all page images + text.\n\nActually, a cleaner approach: render each page to PNG at moderate DPI and send page images + extracted text. The model can read both. But token cost/time.\n\nLet me think about accuracy. The task is adversarial: values scattered, provisional vs authoritative, as-found vs as-left, initial vs final status, checkbox marks. A strong prompt with explicit rules derived from the training set should do well.\n\nLet me first understand all the field semantics